#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kchart; is an application for visualizing numerical data. It has many "
"different chart types available like bar graphs, line plots, pie charts, "
"ring charts and more."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kchart; is a &koffice; component and is very well integrated with &kspread; "
"to allow visualization of spreadsheet data. But it is also possible to use "
"&kchart; as a standalone application or integrate it in other &koffice; "
"components."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:55
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>KDE</keyword>"
msgstr "<keyword>KDE</keyword>"

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid "kdeutils"
msgstr "kdeutils"

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:57
#, no-c-format
msgid "Kchart"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid "chart"
msgstr "diagrama"

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid "graph"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid "plot"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kchart; is a tool for visualizing numerical data. It can be used as a "
"standalone application with a simple &dataeditor;. But as a &koffice; "
"component it offers very flexible integration. &kspread; uses the &kchart; "
"component for charts and diagrams. &kspread; can be seen as a very powerful "
"data provider for &kchart; input."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"But embedding is not limited to &kspread;. A &kchart; chart can be embedded "
"in many of the &koffice; components like &kword;, &kpresenter; or &kivio;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To start with we will look at the user interface of &kchart; and how it can "
"be used as a standalone application. When we are familiar with &kchart; we "
"will investigate the charting capabilities it offers together with &kspread;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid "The User Interface"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:92
#, no-c-format
msgid "Main Application Interface"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"We will take a look at a simple example to get to know &kchart;. Along the "
"way we will also discuss the user interface at length so you will learn "
"about many details of the component."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:98
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you start &kchart; as a standalone application you get the usual "
"startup dialog where you can choose between different templates or load "
"existing chart documents."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kchart; Startup Dialog"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:111
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can choose between various ways to startup &kchart;. On the left, you "
"can see three options: <guilabel>Recent Documents</guilabel>, "
"<guilabel>Charts</guilabel> templates and <guilabel>Open Existing Document..."
"</guilabel>. The first option lets you choose between recently opened "
"charts, the second is for creating a new chart from various templates and "
"the third is for opening an existing chart with a file dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For now we will start with the default template. Select <guilabel>Charts</"
"guilabel> in the left area and then select <guilabel>Bar Chart</guilabel> in "
"the main template area. Normally this template should already be selected "
"after choosing <guilabel>Charts</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:127
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you decide to always start &kchart; with a selected template as default "
"you can also check <guilabel>Always use this template</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Click the <guibutton>Use This Template</guibutton> button on the right, "
"under the template preview."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kchart; User Interface"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:143
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As you can see, there is already some example data present. &kchart; offers "
"the default toolbar for creating a new chart, saving, loading and printing "
"the chart. The second toolbar offers icons for editing the data, configuring "
"the chart or using a configuration wizard. You can also switch between "
"different chart types with the rest of the icons. Note that some of these "
"chart types also have subtypes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The menu offers all standard entries, including shortcut and toolbar "
"configuration and page layout for printing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:155
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that data editing is not available if you use &kchart; from within "
"&kspread; because all data is provided by the spreadsheet. As a standalone "
"application the &dataeditor; is an important part of the application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:163
#, no-c-format
msgid "Chart Wizard"
msgstr "Ajudaire diagrama"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The wizard is actually a very useful part of &kchart; for quickly setting up "
"some basic options like chart type and chart labelling in a few easy steps."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:169
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can run the wizard at any time, it will always preserve your data and "
"other configuration. Also, you can change every single configuration you do "
"with the wizard later as well, without losing anything. In fact the wizard "
"is just a way to set some basic and important options in one go."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:176
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To start the wizard simply click on the <inlinemediaobject><imageobject> "
"<imagedata fileref=\"cr22-action-wizard.png\" format=\"PNG\"/> </"
"imageobject></inlinemediaobject> icon in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step 0 - Choosing the Data source"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:186
#, no-c-format
msgid "Wizard Step 0 - Data source"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:193
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first step in the wizard is to actually choose the data source. If the "
"selected data area does not match the data you want, select the data now."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Include cells that you want to use as row and column labels, if you want "
"them in the chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:200
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Then go to the next step with the <guibutton>Next &gt;</guibutton> button. "
"You can also choose <guibutton>Finish</guibutton> at any step if you are "
"already comfortable with the setup done so far."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step 1 - Choosing the Chart Type"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:210
#, no-c-format
msgid "Wizard Step 1 - Chart Type"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:217
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next step in the wizard is to actually choose the primary type of the "
"chart. This selection has the most important impact on how your data will be "
"presented. Thus it should be chosen well. You can change the type of the "
"chart with the icons in the toolbar without losing any data or configuration "
"later on. In fact it is even considered normal to test all the various chart "
"types to find the best fitting."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:226
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this example we choose the <guilabel>Lines</guilabel> type. Depending on "
"the chosen chart type different options are available in the following steps."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After chosing the type you can get to the next step with the <guibutton>Next "
"&gt;</guibutton> button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:237
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step 2 - Choosing the Subtype"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:239
#, no-c-format
msgid "Wizard Step 2 - Chart Sub Type"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In case the chosen chart type has various sub types you can choose it in the "
"second step. If the chart type has no sub types, this step will be skipped "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:251
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you decide to change the sub type later, you can do this in the "
"appropriate configuration dialog which will be shown later."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can simply leave the default selection and go to the next step. Of "
"course you can chose any desired sub type if you want."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:262
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step 3 - Labels and Legend, Fonts"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:264
#, no-c-format
msgid "Wizard Step 3 - Labels and Legend"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:271
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the third step you can set the text for the chart title, the axes and the "
"legend. You can also configure the desired font in detail for each of these."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:278
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step 4 - Axes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid "Wizard Step 4 - Axes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:287
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The last step of the wizard lets you set various options for the axes and "
"other options, depending on the chart type."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:291
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can either choose <guibutton>Finish</guibutton> to accept the options "
"for your chart, go <guibutton>&lt; Back</guibutton> and change various "
"things or simply dismiss all options from the wizard by choosing "
"<guibutton>Cancel</guibutton>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:297
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you choose <guibutton>Finish</guibutton> all your choices in the wizard "
"will be integrated in the chart and you can continue to enter some data and "
"do some fine tuning of various options."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:302
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Remember that the chart configuration dialog has many more options "
"available, we will discuss these later."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:309
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &dataeditor;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:310
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &dataeditor; can be reached by selecting <menuchoice> <guimenu>Edit</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit Data...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice> from the menu "
"or by clicking on the <inlinemediaobject><imageobject> <imagedata fileref="
"\"cr22-action-data.png\" format=\"PNG\"/> </imageobject></inlinemediaobject> "
"icon in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:319
#, no-c-format
msgid "&dataeditor; in standalone mode"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &dataeditor; can be used to set all values. You can also define the "
"number of rows and columns."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:330
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Depending on the chart type rows and columns have different representations. "
"Each row can generally be considered to be one data series or data set while "
"each column represents the values of all data sets at a certain location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:335
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The name of a data set can be changed by clicking on the row header (left of "
"the first column with values). The name of a column can be changed by "
"clicking on the column header (above the first row)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:341
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The number of rows and columns can be adjusted to fit the needs. Since "
"version 1.4 the restriction to 16 rows and 16 columns has been eliminated."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:348
#, no-c-format
msgid "Chart Configuration"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:349
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kchart; offers many configuration options for the chart. These are "
"available in standalone mode and when embedded in &kspread;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:353
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Depending on the chart type you have selected, the available options are "
"slightly different. Let's choose the line chart type by clicking on the "
"<inlinemediaobject><imageobject> <imagedata fileref=\"cr22-action-chart_line."
"png\" format=\"PNG\"/> </imageobject></inlinemediaobject> icon in the "
"toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:360
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To get to the chart configuration dialog, select <menuchoice> "
"<guimenu>Format</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Chart...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice> "
"from the menu or click on the <inlinemediaobject><imageobject> <imagedata "
"fileref=\"cr22-action-options.png\" format=\"PNG\"/> </imageobject></"
"inlinemediaobject> icon in the toolbar. You might need to click on the right "
"or left arrow at the top of the dialog to get all pages if the width of the "
"dialog is too small."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:371
#, no-c-format
msgid "Row and Column Swapping"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:373
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 1 - Row/column swapping"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:380
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first configuration page can be used to swap the interpretation of rows "
"and columns. By default one row is considered to be a data set and each "
"column holds the individual values of the data series. Here you can choose "
"to have a each column hold one data set. Note that the values are not really "
"swapped but only their interpretation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:391
#, no-c-format
msgid "Chart Subtype"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:393
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 2 - Chart Subtype"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:400
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The second page can be used to select the desired sub type of a chart. The "
"available sub types depend on the chart type, in this case the line chart. "
"Some chart types have no sub type at all in which case this configuration "
"page is not shown. You also get a preview for each subtype."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:407
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Remember that the chart type can be chosen from the toolbar while the "
"subtype can be set through this configuration dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:413
#, no-c-format
msgid "Header and Footer"
msgstr "Entèsta e pè de pagina"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:415
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 3 - Header and Footer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:422
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the third page you can set the title, the subtitle and the footer of the "
"chart, each with individual font settings."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:428
#, no-c-format
msgid "Legend"
msgstr "Legenda"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:430
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 4 - Legend"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:437
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The legend configuration page lets you set all aspects of the legend. The "
"legend contains the names for all data sets with the respective colors, this "
"is important to identify the data on the chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:442
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>General</guilabel> box holds the title of the legend, which is "
"displayed at the top of the legend box. The <guilabel>Position</guilabel> "
"box can be used to place the legend at various locations on the chart. Use "
"the central button to hide the legend."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:448
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Font</guilabel> box can be used to set different fonts for the "
"legend title and the individual entries. Additionally you can set different "
"colors in the <guilabel>Color</guilabel> box."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:455
#, no-c-format
msgid "Axes"
msgstr "Axes"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:457
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 5 - Axes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:464
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Axes</guilabel> page holds configuration for the chart axes. "
"This page highly depends on the chart type chosen. For the line chart you "
"can set linear or logarithmic scales and turn the grid on and off."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:472
#, no-c-format
msgid "Colors"
msgstr "Colors"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:474
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 6 - Colors"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:481
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the <guilabel>Colors</guilabel> page you can choose the colors for the "
"individual data sets. You can also set colors for some general chart parts "
"like axes <guilabel>Line color</guilabel> and <guilabel>Grid color</"
"guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:488
#, no-c-format
msgid "Font"
msgstr "Poliça"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:490
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 7 - Font"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:497
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Font</guilabel> page can be used to configure various fonts "
"like titles, axes labels and so on. Some of these fonts can be set on other "
"pages as well, but on the font page you have them all in one place."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:505
#, no-c-format
msgid "Background"
msgstr "Fons"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:507
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration page 8 - Background"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:514
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the <guilabel>Background</guilabel> page you will find various options "
"for tweaking the background settings of the chart. You can either choose "
"different background colors or even a background picture."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:521
#, no-c-format
msgid "More..."
msgstr "Mai..."

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:522
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This short overview of the configuration options gave you an introduction to "
"the configuration possibilities of your chart, it is by no means complete. "
"Different chart types like <guilabel>Bar</guilabel>, <guilabel>Line</"
"guilabel> and <guilabel>Pie</guilabel>, have additional specialised "
"configuration pages and you are welcome to look around, try different chart "
"types and look at the configuration possibilities. Use the tooltips and read "
"the What's This? help. To use the What's This? help simply click on the "
"question mark on the window decoration and then click on the area of the "
"configuration page you want get more information on."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:540
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kchart; as a standalone Application"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:541
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The previous chapter described the user interface, various configuration "
"options, data editing and the wizard in detail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:546
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This chapter gives real-life examples on how to use &kchart; in various "
"situations as a standalone application. The purpose is to give you some "
"understanding of the way &kchart; works and how various parts relate to each "
"other."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:553
#, no-c-format
msgid "Presenting Sales Figures"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:555
#, no-c-format
msgid "Warming up"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:556
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This first example which might often be encountered in real life is "
"presenting sales figures, or in this particular case profit."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:561
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Imagine you own a company that has three main categories of business: sales, "
"support and training. And you would like to present the profit of these "
"categories over some years. Making a graphical statistics is certainly a "
"good idea. &kchart; can help you here."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:570
#, no-c-format
msgid "Getting started"
msgstr "Per començar"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:571
#, no-c-format
msgid "Run &kchart; and select the <guilabel>Blank Worksheet</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:575
#, no-c-format
msgid "Starting with a blank chart"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:582
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Hit the button <guibutton>Use This Template</guibutton> to get started. "
"&kchart; will present to you a completely empty document. That's good, we "
"just need to enter some data and do some basic configuration to get a nice "
"chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:588
#, no-c-format
msgid "A blank chart"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:597
#, no-c-format
msgid "Getting the numbers in"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:598
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first step, and probably the most important, is to get the actual data "
"into the chart. This can easily be done with the &dataeditor;. As usual you "
"can get to the data editor by clicking on the "
"<inlinemediaobject><imageobject> <imagedata fileref=\"cr22-action-data.png\" "
"format=\"PNG\"/> </imageobject></inlinemediaobject> icon in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:606
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You will notice that no data is present. Also note that the number of data "
"rows and columns is set to a minimum, which is 1."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:611
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Now, just enter the data you see in the next screenshot. Note that you can "
"increase the number of rows and columns with the spin boxes in the lower "
"left corner. The names of the rows and columns themselves can be changed by "
"clicking on them."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:619
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Data"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:626
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Simply click the <guibutton>OK</guibutton> button when you are done. This "
"will present to you a simple bar chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:631
#, no-c-format
msgid "Simple chart without fine tuning"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:638
#, no-c-format
msgid "Read on to do some fine tuning!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:643
#, no-c-format
msgid "Fine Tuning"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:644
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"We will now do some fine tuning and put a title on the chart. And, we will "
"add a nice 3D effect to the bars as well."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:648
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Start up the configuration dialog by clicking on the "
"<inlinemediaobject><imageobject> <imagedata fileref=\"cr22-action-options.png"
"\" format=\"PNG\"/> </imageobject></inlinemediaobject> icon in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:655
#, no-c-format
msgid "Title"
msgstr "Títol"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:657
#, no-c-format
msgid "Options - Title"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:664
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let's start with labeling. Go to the <guilabel>Header/Footer</guilabel> page "
"and enter the text like in the above screenshot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:670
#, no-c-format
msgid "X-Axis Font Settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:671
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next step is to make the font of the x axis a bit bigger. You can do "
"this on the <guilabel>Font</guilabel> page."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:676
#, no-c-format
msgid "Options - Fonts"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:683
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Choose the <guilabel>X-Axis</guilabel> item and click on the "
"<guibutton>Font...</guibutton> button. Choose a somewhat larger font like in "
"the following screenshot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:689
#, no-c-format
msgid "Options - X-Axis Font"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:696
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that the font size is set to <guilabel>Relative</guilabel>. This means "
"that the font is automatically scaled according to the overall chart size. "
"This is quite usefull and most of the time what the average user expects."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:704
#, no-c-format
msgid "Giving it a 3D Look"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:705
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some types of charts like <guilabel>Bar</guilabel> and <guilabel>Pie</"
"guilabel> have an additional configuration page."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:710
#, no-c-format
msgid "Options - 3D Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:717
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To get a 3D effect for this type of chart, go to the <guilabel>Bar</"
"guilabel> page and simply activate <guilabel>3D bar</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:721
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"That's all what we need for a reasonable looking chart. Note that much of "
"this configuration could also have been achieved with the wizard. It's "
"basically a matter of taste what you use. Experienced users will likely use "
"the full option dialog we used in this example."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:728
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Simply accept the settings by clicking on the <guibutton>OK</guibutton> "
"button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:735
#, no-c-format
msgid "Final output"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:736
#, no-c-format
msgid "The final chart will look like the next screenshot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:740
#, no-c-format
msgid "Final Chart"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:747
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You might want to enlarge the application window to see the chart in full "
"size. Note how the fonts get larger in relation to the chart size."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:752
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are certainly aspects to improve further for your personal taste. For "
"example try to make the title font even bigger. Just experiment further, "
"this way you will get to know a lot of the &kchart; application."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:758
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Finally you can save the chart from the <guimenu>File</guimenu> menu and "
"quit &kchart; with <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Quit</"
"guimenuitem> </menuchoice>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:767
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Exporting to Graphic Formats: SVG, PNG, JPG, &krita;, &karbon14;, Gimp and "
"more"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:769
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For further processing, the chart can also be exported as a graphics file. "
"Many formats are available. Using either PNG, SVG, JPG or &krita; will "
"likely produce the best result."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:774
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To export your current chart simply choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu> <guimenuitem>Export...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> from the menu and "
"select the desired file format from the filter box."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:793
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kchart; in &kspread;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:795
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In addition to standalone operation, &kchart; is designed to be used with "
"&kspread;. This chapter describes how to create and manipulate charts from "
"within &kspread;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:800
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Remember that &kchart; embedding into &kspread; is a very commonly used and "
"well implemented feature, the examples in this chapter should just get you "
"started."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:805
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As soon as you have created a chart in &kspread; you can take full advantage "
"of all the &kchart; features by double clicking on the chart area. This is "
"necessary to get to all advanced configuration options. Keep in mind that "
"the data editor is not available when &kchart; is embedded into &kspread;. "
"This is obvious since &kspread; serves, from &kchart;'s point of view, as a "
"powerful data provider."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:814
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Swapping x and y is of particular interest when working with &kspread;. You "
"can swap interpretation of x and y axis in &kchart; in the Data Format "
"settings which can be accessed through the Edit menu or the context menu "
"when right clicking on the chart itself. As noted above you need to be "
"inside the &kchart; component in order to access these settings, which can "
"be done by double clicking the embedded chart in your spreadsheet."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:825
#, no-c-format
msgid "Plotting with &kchart;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:826
#, no-c-format
msgid "To plot with &kchart;, when it is embedded in &kspread;, do the following:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:829 index.docbook:863 index.docbook:1264 index.docbook:1279
#, no-c-format
msgid "Picture of toolbars dialog"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:838
#, no-c-format
msgid "Highlight the second row of numbers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:842
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Next click on the <guilabel>Chart</guilabel> button "
"<inlinemediaobject><imageobject><imagedata fileref=\"frame_chart.png\" "
"format=\"PNG\"/></imageobject></inlinemediaobject> to create the chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:848
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Select the options you want from the chart wizard. The chart Wizard will pop "
"up after you insert the chart."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:853
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The whole data could also be created using two columns instead of two rows. "
"If using columns you might want to switch x and y axis in the &kchart; "
"configuration as described above."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:859
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To make a bar chart for individual items, put the numbers in a column, as "
"shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:871
#, no-c-format
msgid "The orientation of the numbers determines how the plot will be made."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:875
#, no-c-format
msgid "A row of numbers defines the ordinates for a single plot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:879
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A column of numbers defines the heights for each bar, in a bar graph. It "
"also determines the size of the slices in a pie chart."
msgstr ""

